<h2>The transaction and the trial</h2>
<p>The complainant&rsquo;s case was simple: Rs 7 lakh advanced through banking channels, two cheques of Rs 4 lakh and Rs 3 lakh issued in repayment, both dishonoured. The trial court convicted under Section 138 NI Act, imposing six months&rsquo; simple imprisonment and a Rs 10 lakh fine with default imprisonment. The first appellate court affirmed. In revision, the petitioners pressed two stories: the cheques were security for a tenancy, and the complainant&rsquo;s transfers were actually payment for sanitary goods supplied.</p>

<h2>Where the defence broke</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Silence at the notice stage.</strong> Neither story appeared in reply to the statutory demand notice, nor at the Section 251 CrPC stage when notice was framed. Defences that materialise only at trial start life discounted.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>An inadmissible document.</strong> The lease deed (DW1/A) was an unstamped, unnotarised photocopy. The original was never produced and no foundation for secondary evidence was laid, so the photocopy proved nothing — and the petitioners, who admitted other leases were properly executed, offered no explanation for this one&rsquo;s defects.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Conduct inconsistent with the story.</strong> The petitioners did not protest the dishonour or seek return of the cheques — conduct the appellate court held irreconcilable with the claim that the instruments were mere security.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Proved consideration.</strong> The complainant&rsquo;s transfers were through banking channels, deflating the challenge to his financial capacity.</div>
</div>
<p>With signatures admitted, the presumptions under Sections 118 and 139 NI Act operated at full strength, and the trial court&rsquo;s observation — quoted in the judgment — captured the standard: mere production of a disputed lease deed, without proof, does not dislodge the presumption.</p>

<h2>The revisional threshold</h2>
<p>The judgment is equally instructive on forum discipline. After conviction and a failed appeal, the High Court&rsquo;s revisional jurisdiction under the BNSS is supervisory: it corrects jurisdictional error, illegality and perversity, and it does not conduct a third round of fact-finding. The petitioners&rsquo; inability to show that the concurrent findings were erroneous or contrary to the record was, by itself, the end of the matter. The Court declined to interfere, dismissed the revision, and directed the petitioners to appear before the trial court on 20 November 2026 to pay the compensation and undergo the sentence — the earlier extension of the surrender date having run out.</p>

<h2>Lessons for drawers and payees</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>If a cheque is truly security, say so at the first opportunity — in the reply to the demand notice — and demand its return in writing when the underlying arrangement ends.</p>
  <p>Documents win these cases: an unstamped photocopy with no original is worse than no document, because it advertises the defence&rsquo;s weakness.</p>
  <p>For complainants, route every advance through banking channels; proof of capacity and consideration then takes care of itself.</p>
  <p>Treat the revision stage realistically: it is a forum for legal error, not a second appeal on facts.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article reports a judgment for general information only and is not legal advice in any pending cheque-dishonour matter.</div>
